<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Aleijadinho</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1738–<a href="/years/1814">1814</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Antônio Francisco Lisboa</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Aleijadinho, born Antônio Francisco Lisboa, was a sculptor and architect of colonial <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minas-gerais">Minas Gerais</a> and the leading artist of the Brazilian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a>. The son of a Portuguese architect and an enslaved African woman, he worked on churches in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ouro-preto">Ouro Preto</a> and other mining towns. Despite a disabling illness, he carved the soapstone prophets at the sanctuary of Congonhas.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aleijadinho.jpg" alt="Aleijadinho" width="320" height="422" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Euclásio Ventura · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Suposto_retrato_de_Aleijadinho_(cropped).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Antônio Francisco Lisboa, called Aleijadinho, was the son of a respected Portuguese master builder and architect, Manuel Francisco Lisboa, and his African slave Isabel. The baptismal certificate cited by his biographer Rodrigo José Ferreira Bretas says he was born a slave and baptised on 29 August 1730 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ouro-preto">Vila Rica</a>, now Ouro Preto, and manumitted on that occasion by his father; the date of birth is not given, and arguments now favour a later year.</p><p>Little is known with certainty about his life. Almost all the available data derive from Bretas's biography of 1858, written 44 years after his death, which recent criticism considers largely fanciful, part of a romanticised process that made him an icon of Brazilianness.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1756 he may have gone to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-janeiro">Rio de Janeiro</a> with Friar Lucas de Santa Clara, who transported gold and diamonds, and may have been influenced there by local artists. Two years later he would have made a soapstone fountain for the Terra Santa Hospice and then set up as a self-employed professional. Being a mulatto, he was often forced to accept contracts as a day labourer rather than as a master.</p><p>From the 1760s until close to his death he produced many works, but without supporting documentation many are only attributions based on stylistic similarity with documented pieces. His work was carried out entirely in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minas-gerais">Minas Gerais</a>, especially in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ouro-preto">Ouro Preto</a>, Sabará, São João del-Rei and Congonhas, notably the Church of Saint <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-of-assisi">Francis of Assisi</a> in Ouro Preto.</p><p>In 1796 he received a commission of great importance, the sculptures of the Via Sacra and the Prophets for the Sanctuary of Bom Jesus of Matosinhos in Congonhas, considered his masterpiece. Between 1807 and 1809, his illness at an advanced stage, he closed his workshop but still carried out some work, and from 1812 his health deteriorated further. He died on 18 November 1814.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Aleijadinho worked in the transition from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a> to Rococo, and his work reflects both. Artists of the time did not sign their works, so identification relies on contracts and receipts between religious brotherhoods and artists, on other documents and oral tradition, and on stylistic comparison, which is always conjectural. More than four hundred creations are associated with his name, most attributed without documents.</p><p>His documented gilded woodcarving includes at least four large altarpieces, in which the crowning arch, without a pediment, is replaced by a statuary group, which suggests a primarily sculptural vocation. Brazilian critics almost by consensus consider him the foremost exponent of colonial art in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>, and some foreign scholars the leading name of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Aleijadinho” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Wealthy brotherhoods built baroque churches decorated by the sculptor <b>Aleijadinho</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/captaincy-of-minas-gerais">Captaincy of Minas Gerais</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Cachoeira do Campo, in what is now <a href="/countries/brazil">Brazil</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ouro-preto">Ouro Preto</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/brazil">Brazil</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-20.35,-43.67&amp;year=1787" aria-label="Cachoeira do Campo on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Aleijadinho"><image href="/maps/globes/g330s30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3914 4243h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3914 4243h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=-20.35,-43.67&amp;year=1787">What was here in 1787? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
